---
import ContentGrid from "../structure/ContentGrid.astro";
import ContentSection from "../structure/ContentSection.astro";
import ExhibitGroup from "../content/ExhibitGroup.astro";
import ExhibitItem from "../content/ExhibitItem.astro";
import panelImage from "../../../images/banners/cv_sarah_panel_ogt.jpg";
import BannerCaption from "../content/BannerCaption.astro";
---

<ContentSection id="exhibits" title="Exhibits & Museum History" headingLevel="h2">
<ContentSection id="working-museum" title="A working museum" headingLevel="h3">
      <ContentGrid columns={2}>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            The museum sits inside a working telephone company central office in
            Georgetown, a building where calls are connected and routed.
            Volunteers keep switches that once connected whole
            neighborhoods ready to carry calls again. Hear the relays, watch the
            machinery move, and ask the person who works on it what just happened.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Herb Warrick put the idea for a telephone museum on paper in 1984,
            as the Bell System was breaking up and older switches were nearing
            retirement. Pacific Northwest Bell made room for the equipment and
            helped with the heavy frames. Don Ostrand and Telephone Pioneer
            volunteers began building the museum here in 1986, laying out,
            moving, and reconnecting the huge switch frames.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
      </ContentGrid>

      <p>
        You might find volunteers on ladders, pulling cable, soldering, testing,
        or chasing a fault. Keeping these switches working was a full-time job
        when they carried everyday calls, and it still takes skilled hands.
        Experienced telephone workers and newer volunteers pass that knowledge
        along as they keep the machines running. The big switches anchor the
        rooms, while smaller displays move and new functions come online. Take
        a tour or explore the public areas at your own pace; our volunteer
        docents are glad to talk through what you're seeing and what's changed
        since your last visit.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

<ContentSection id="follow-a-call" title="Follow a call across both floors" headingLevel="h3">
      <p>
        Lift a receiver and dial. Someone or something has to find your line,
        understand the number, choose a route, and ring the other phone. A
        switchboard operator once made connections by hand. Ask a volunteer
        docent to trace that work through a machine on the third floor, then
        take the same question downstairs: how did engineers fit more of it
        into less room?
      </p>
      <ContentGrid columns={2}>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Upstairs, step-by-step switches climb and turn with dial pulses.
            The 1923 Panel stores dialed digits in a sender, then drives its
            selectors with motors. Crossbar switches connect at intersections
            of metal bars. You can hear and see much of the logic at work in
            the aisles.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Downstairs, compare the 3ESS and DMS-10. Transistors and later
            integrated circuits packed more control into circuit cards and
            processors. Across the two floors, you can trace the move from
            room-filling mechanisms to smaller machines with more functions.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
      </ContentGrid>
      <p>
        The big switches stay put, but available call paths and demonstrations
        can change as volunteers repair, wire, and test them.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ExhibitGroup
      id="central-office-switches"
      title="Mechanical switching"
      headingLevel="h3"
      columns={2}
    >
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="Panel switch"
          headingLevel="h4"
          image={panelImage}
          alt="Chief Engineer Sarah Autumn beside the museum's panel switching equipment"
          imageCaption="Chief Engineer Sarah Autumn at the Panel switch."
          imageSizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 33vw"
          href="#first-dial-service"
          linkLabel="Read the Panel's story"
        >
          <p>
            Seattle's 1923 Panel switch still connects calls. A sender takes
            in the number you dial; motor-driven rods move brushes across
            banks of contacts to find a path to the other phone. This working
            piece of the old Parkway office survived a heavy move and years of
            volunteer rewiring.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="No. 1 Crossbar"
          headingLevel="h4"
          href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Number_One_Crossbar_Switch"
          linkLabel="Read the No. 1 Crossbar's history"
        >
          <p>
            In a crossbar switch, relays choose a route and horizontal and
            vertical bars close the contacts that connect it. More than 100
            Western Electric workers built this switch for northeast Seattle's
            Vermont office, where it began carrying calls in 1942. Museum
            founder Herb Warrick worked there as a switchman. After the
            switch came here, volunteers rebuilt its wiring and brought more
            of its call paths to life. In 2017, they got a call through to it
            from the Panel again. It's the only No. 1 Crossbar still known to
            be operating.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask a volunteer docent about blue boxing: early phone phreaks
            used tones to signal switches. Which demonstrations you can try
            depends on what's running that day.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="No. 5 Crossbar"
          headingLevel="h4"
          href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Number_Five_Crossbar_Switch_(5XB)"
          linkLabel="See how the No. 5 Crossbar keeps growing"
        >
          <p>
            Our No. 5 entered service at Mercer Island's Adams office in
            1958. Switchman Don Fagerholm helped choose and install the
            frames that came here. Volunteers have since added functions,
            from renewed links to the Panel to working coin-call circuits.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem title="Step-by-step and PBXs" headingLevel="h4">
          <p>
            Step-by-step switches respond to each pulse from a rotary dial.
            The museum's community dial office combines equipment from
            Crystal Mountain, Sumner, and Belfair. Smaller private branch
            exchanges, or PBXs, connect phones within a building and out to
            the wider network.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
    </ExhibitGroup>

    <ExhibitGroup
      id="power-and-ringing"
      title="Keeping the switches running"
      headingLevel="h3"
      columns={2}
    >
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="Power board"
          headingLevel="h4"
          href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Power_Board"
          linkLabel="Read the power board's equipment notes"
        >
          <p>
            The switches need a steady supply of electricity. The power board
            distributes 48-volt office battery power to the museum's equipment,
            with meters and fuses that let volunteers watch what the machinery
            is drawing. This board came from Seattle's Parkway central office;
            volunteers have adapted it for the museum.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="Ringing and tone plant"
          headingLevel="h4"
          href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:803C_Ringing_and_Tone_Plant"
          linkLabel="See how the ringing and tone plant was rebuilt"
        >
          <p>
            This equipment makes the ringing current and tones used by the panel
            and No. 1 Crossbar. Volunteers brought together a motor generator
            from Seattle's West office and a control board from Hartford,
            Connecticut, then wired them into a working exhibit. A speaker lets
            visitors hear the tones for themselves.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
    </ExhibitGroup>

    <ExhibitGroup
      id="electronic-switching"
      title="Electronic switching"
      headingLevel="h3"
      columns={2}
    >
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="Western Electric 3ESS"
          headingLevel="h4"
          href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:3ESS"
          linkLabel="Read how volunteers brought the 3ESS back up"
        >
          <p>
            This electronic switch served Crosby, Washington, from 1980 to
            1994. Its boot tapes failed at the museum; volunteers recovered
            the data and built an emulator so it could start again. It
            returned to service in 2021. Keeping an electronic machine alive
            takes more than a power cord.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ExhibitItem
          title="DMS-10"
          headingLevel="h4"
          href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:DMS-10"
          linkLabel="Follow the DMS-10's trip to Seattle"
        >
          <p>
            Built around 2001, this digital switch uses PowerPC processors,
            hard drives, and memory. Volunteers brought it from an Alabama
            lab in 2023, then craned it into the museum and wired it up. Beside
            the 3ESS, it shows how much switching could fit in a smaller
            cabinet.
          </p>
        </ExhibitItem>
      </r-cell>
    </ExhibitGroup>

    <ContentSection id="learn-more" title="Want to go deeper?" headingLevel="h3">
      <p>
        If you'd like the nuts and bolts behind the tour, read more about
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telephone_exchange">telephone exchanges</a>,
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossbar_switch">crossbar switches</a>,
        and the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panel_switch">Panel switch</a>.
        Museum volunteers have helped write and expand these articles.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

<BannerCaption image={panelImage} alt="Sarah Autumn beside the working Panel switch and outgoing trunk test desk">
      Chief Engineer Sarah Autumn beside the Panel switch and its outgoing trunk
      test desk.
    </BannerCaption>

    <ContentSection id="first-dial-service" title="Seattle starts dialing" headingLevel="h3">
      <p>
        At midnight on March 3, 1923, Seattle's Rainier exchange and two other
        offices began the city's first dial telephone service. The Rainier
        switch, later called Parkway, started with about 4,000 lines. For a
        caller, dialing could now set the connection in motion. Behind the walls,
        it took a room of motors, relays, selectors, and people who knew how to
        keep them working.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Panel served the Rainier/Parkway office into the 1970s. The museum
        has a working portion of the original installation; the exchange it
        came from was about eight times larger. It is the only surviving
        working Panel switch, which makes keeping its skills alive as
        important as keeping its parts.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection id="watch-a-call" title="Watch it find a path" headingLevel="h3">
      <ContentGrid columns={2}>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Lifting the receiver closes a circuit to the central office. A line
            finder locates your line and connects it to a sender, which supplies
            dial tone and stores the digits you dial. A decoder helps turn those
            digits into a route. On a final frame, motor-driven rods carry
            brushes up banks of contacts until the switch reaches the called
            line. It's a lot of movement for one ringing phone.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Unlike a step-by-step switch, the dial pulses don't directly move
            each selector. The sender counts and stores them, then guides the
            selectors and checks their progress. Once the talking path is set,
            the sender is free for another call. A small group of senders could
            therefore set up calls for many lines. The Panel's decoder handled
            the routing lookup, a job later crossbar switches gave to circuits
            called markers.
            Listen for the motors and ask a volunteer docent which part has your
            call now.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
      </ContentGrid>
      <p>
        The outgoing trunk test desk beside the switch is part of that working
        story. Technicians used test desks and routine checks to find trouble
        before it reached callers. Volunteers still trace faults and keep the
        circuits in order here.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection id="the-move" title="A switch that had to leave through a window" headingLevel="h3">
      <p>
        When the Panel retired, it stayed in the Parkway building. Removing it
        right away could have disturbed cables serving the No. 1 Crossbar on
        the floor above. That delay helped save it. Volunteers selected frames
        for the new museum in 1986. The following year, they took them out
        through an exterior window because the doors were too small, then
        carried them across town on several flatbed trips.
      </p>
      <p>
        Emery Schaffer and Norm Christiansen, with other volunteers, assembled
        the frames here and got calls running again. Later caretakers kept it
        going. Since 2014, Sarah Autumn has rewired the connections between
        frames, rebuilt the routing, and brought more parts of the switch back
        into service. The Panel is a working machine because people still trace
        wires, find faults, and teach each other how it works.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection id="keep-learning" title="Keep following the circuit" headingLevel="h3">
      <p>
        Ask a volunteer docent what the Panel is doing today. Restoration and
        maintenance continue, so a tour may catch a new function, a test in
        progress, or a problem someone is chasing down.
      </p>
      <p>
        For frame lists, diagrams, and the longer equipment history, read the
        museum's <a href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Panel_Switch">Panel switch inventory</a>
        or the Telecommunications History Group's
        <a href="https://www.telcomhistory.org/seattleSwitching.html">mechanical switching exhibit</a>.
        The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panel_switch">Panel switch article on Wikipedia</a>
        goes deeper into senders, selectors, testing, and the system's history.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

<p>
      The big switches anchor the rooms. Around them, you'll find the phones,
      teletypes, and tools that helped people use and maintain the network.
      Smaller displays may move as volunteers work on them.
    </p>

    <ContentSection
      id="telephones-and-station-equipment"
      title="Payphones and other telephones"
      headingLevel="h3"
    >
      <!-- TODO(issue): Review Claire's museum-signage payphone copy for details to add here. PR #13 left this draft placeholder: "[[ use Claire's payphone copy from the museum signage here.]]" -->
      <p>
        Before a phone lived in nearly every pocket, a public phone could be
        how you called home, found a ride, or reported trouble. Our second-floor
        payphone collection brings together machines from several decades.
        Look at the coin slots, keypads, and housings, and ask a volunteer
        docent how a payphone call was handled by a central-office switch.
        You'll also find switchboards and telephones from many eras.
      </p>
      <p>
        The coin is part of the engineering story, too. Chief Engineer Sarah
        Autumn and Steve Flocke brought coin-call functions back to the No. 5
        Crossbar. Flocke designed a new circuit that supervises what happens
        to the coins.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ask about the cast-iron British K-6 call box. A Seattle volunteer and
        fellow radio amateurs helped bring it from Norwich, England. It rode
        to Boeing Field on a cargo flight carrying jet engines, then went up
        to the museum's second floor on a boom truck. It's a good story about
        the people and practical work behind a collection.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.telcomhistory.org/seattleTelephones.html">Read the K-6 call box story</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Other phones tell different stories. The Space Age Cordless Telephone
        made calls by radio from the Space Needle during the 1962 World's
        Fair; our collection also includes original Picturephones. Ask which
        sets are available to try when you visit.
        <a href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Space_Age_Cordless_Telephone">Read about the Space Needle phone</a>.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection
      id="other-exhibit-categories"
      title="Telegraphs and teletypes"
      headingLevel="h3"
    >
      <p>
        Long before a text appeared on a screen, teletypes sent typed messages
        over a line and printed them on paper. Our collection spans machines
        from the 1920s through the 1980s, including a Model 15 that once
        worked for the Associated Press. News services used machines like
        these to get stories to newspapers and broadcasters. At the museum,
        volunteers can patch some of the teletypes together at a testboard
        or feed them news and weather. One of our machines has also gone out
        to film productions, including <cite>Argo</cite>. Look for the
        telegraph keys and sounders nearby, too.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.telcomhistory.org/seattleTelegraphs.html">Read the older telegraph and teletype exhibit</a>
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection id="outside-plant" title="Beyond the switchroom" headingLevel="h3">
      <p>
        Cable splicers and line crews built and repaired the network that
        carried calls in and out. Our outside-plant display has examples of
        their tools, wires, and splices. Its arrangement may change as
        volunteers work on the collection.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>
</ContentSection>
